Hi all,

I work on microsatellites in fungi (method STRAf in Aspergillus fumigatus). These are 9 microsatellites (called 2A, 2B, 2C, 3A, 3B, 3C, 4A, 4B, 4C) with repeats of 2,2,2,3,3,3 and 4,4,4 nucleotides respectively.

I give an example of data for one strain:

STRAf 2A: a fragment of 170 bp in this microsat = 14 repetitions of dinucleotides

STRAf 2B: 146 bp in this microsat = 21 repetitions of dinucleotides

STRAf 2C: 153 bp in this microsat = 8 repetitions of dinucleotides

STRAf 3A: 208 bp in this microsat = 33 repetitions of trinucleotides

My question is (perhaps trivial but many of us are using this and we don't agree.):

For Bruvo’s distance, should we work on the size of the fragments in base pairs or data transformed in the number of repetitions on each microsatellite (always presented like this in papers).

I looked at the example with data(nancycats). “the data presented is assuming that the nancycat dataset contains all dinucleotide repeats”.
Are the values fca8, fca23 etc.. microsat ? They go each time from 2 to 2. So I deduce that they are base pairs unit.
And that’s why the example is :
“# Assume the alleles are all dinucleotide repeats.
ssr <- rep(2, nLoc(nancycats)) “

For our method, some authors used repeat numbers and ran the script:

bruvo.dist(pop, c(2,2,2,3,3,3,4,4,4))

But it’s aggregate strains that are different (until 16 base pairs on STRAf 4A for example).

I've done various tests on fictitious data. For me, if we use repetition numbers I would use bruvo.dist(pop, rep(1,9)).

But I'd like your opinion...

I hope my message is clear enough.

All the best

Steffi

